### What is the defining difference?

The defining difference is directionality. WebSocket is full-duplex: after the HTTP upgrade handshake, both client and server send messages at any time, independently. SSE is half-duplex: the server streams events to the client, and the client cannot send on that channel.

```text
WebSocket:  Client ←──messages──→ Server  (both ways)
SSE:        Client ←──events──── Server   (server to client only)
```

### When do you need WebSocket?

Use WebSocket when the interaction is genuinely two-way and interactive: a chat where both people type, a multiplayer game, collaborative document editing, or a trading terminal where orders go up and fills come down.

```java
package com.bank.websocket;

import org.springframework.web.socket.*;
import org.springframework.web.socket.handler.TextWebSocketHandler;

public class TradingHandler extends TextWebSocketHandler {

    @Override
    protected void handleTextMessage(WebSocketSession session, TextMessage message) {
        Order order = parseOrder(message.getPayload());
        Fill fill = matchEngine.execute(order);
        session.sendMessage(new TextMessage(toJson(fill)));
        // Two-way: client sends orders, server sends fills
    }
}
```

### When is SSE the better fit?

Use SSE when the flow is only server-to-client: live notifications, stock tickers, monitoring dashboards, progress updates. SSE runs over plain HTTP, passes through proxies and firewalls, and the browser's `EventSource` reconnects automatically.

```java
package com.bank.controller;

import org.springframework.http.MediaType;
import org.springframework.web.servlet.mvc.method.annotation.SseEmitter;

@GetMapping(value = "/api/v1/notifications/{userId}",
            produces = MediaType.TEXT_EVENT_STREAM_VALUE)
public SseEmitter notifications(@PathVariable String userId) {
    SseEmitter emitter = new SseEmitter(0L);
    notificationHub.subscribe(userId, event ->
            emitter.send(SseEmitter.event().id(event.id()).data(event)));
    return emitter;
}
```

```javascript
// Browser: 3 lines, auto-reconnect, resume from last event ID:
const es = new EventSource('/api/v1/notifications/rajesh');
es.onmessage = (e) => showNotification(JSON.parse(e.data));
// If connection drops, EventSource reconnects and sends Last-Event-ID header
```

### How do WebSocket and SSE compare? (comparison table)

| Feature | WebSocket | SSE |
|---|---|---|
| Direction | Full-duplex (both ways) | Server → client only |
| Protocol | Upgraded TCP (ws/wss) | Plain HTTP |
| Auto-reconnect | Manual | Built-in (EventSource) |
| Resume from last event | Manual | Built-in (Last-Event-ID) |
| Binary support | Yes | No (text only) |
| Proxy friendliness | May be blocked | Passes through |
| Infrastructure | WebSocket-aware required | Standard HTTP |
| Per-message overhead | Very low (2-14 bytes) | Low (text framing) |
| Complexity | Higher | Lower |

### What about SSE's limitations?

SSE is text-only and cannot send binary data. Over HTTP/1.1, browsers limit concurrent SSE connections per domain (typically 6), which HTTP/2 multiplexing solves. And the client cannot send data back on the same channel.

```java
// SSE limitation: client cannot send on the stream.
// If the client needs to acknowledge a notification, use a separate REST call:
@PostMapping("/api/v1/notifications/{id}/acknowledge")
public void acknowledge(@PathVariable String id) {
    notificationService.markRead(id);
}
// The SSE stream stays one-way; the acknowledgment is a normal POST.
```

### What about WebSocket's costs?

WebSocket needs WebSocket-aware load balancers and proxies. You must implement reconnection and heartbeats yourself. You lose HTTP caching. And you must manage the connection lifecycle for long-lived connections at scale.

```java
// WebSocket: must implement heartbeat yourself:
@Scheduled(fixedRate = 30000)
public void heartbeat() {
    sessions.forEach(session -> {
        try {
            session.sendMessage(new PingMessage());
        } catch (IOException e) {
            sessions.remove(session); // dead connection
        }
    });
}
```

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is using WebSocket for a one-way feed and then carrying the complexity of reconnection, heartbeats, and WebSocket-aware infrastructure for something SSE handles automatically.

```java
// Wrong – WebSocket for one-way stock prices:
// Must implement: reconnection, heartbeats, lifecycle, load balancer config

// Correct – SSE for one-way stock prices:
@GetMapping(value = "/prices", produces = MediaType.TEXT_EVENT_STREAM_VALUE)
public SseEmitter prices() {
    SseEmitter emitter = new SseEmitter(0L);
    priceFeed.subscribe(price -> emitter.send(price));
    return emitter;
}
// Auto-reconnect, proxy-friendly, 3 lines on the client
```

### How do you handle the "mostly one-way" case?

If a feature is mostly server-push but needs an occasional client action (like acknowledging a notification), keep the SSE stream one-way and make the client action a normal REST call. This keeps the streaming channel simple.

```text
  SSE stream:    Server ──notifications──→ Client (one-way)
  REST action:   Client ──POST /ack──→ Server (separate call)
  // Simple and clean – no need for WebSocket
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer defaults to SSE for server-push and reaches for WebSocket only when the client must push in real time. They configure SSE event IDs so the browser can resume from the last received event after reconnection. They set `SseEmitter` timeout to `0L` for indefinite streams and handle `onCompletion` and `onError` callbacks to clean up subscriptions. In banking, notifications, tickers, and dashboards are overwhelmingly one-way, while trading terminals and live chat support are two-way.
